## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates how a manager translates reliability goals into a concrete plan, balancing speed and risk, across teams and regions.

## Key Concepts
- SLOs and SLIs for latency and errors
- Incident response and runbooks
- Canary deployments and feature flags
- Tail latency and multi-region observability
- Instrumentation, profiling, and postmortems

## Code Example
```javascript
function percentile(arr, p){
  if (arr.length===0) return 0;
  const s = arr.slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const idx = Math.floor((p/100)*(s.length-1));
  return s[idx];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you triage if p95 spikes are caused by an upstream service?
- How would you communicate risk to leadership during an incident?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests balancing reliability and speed in a multi-team setting with a live data pipeline. It pushes candidates to articulate ownership, metrics, and governance for a delicate system upgrade.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architecture, exactly-once delivery, idempotency
- Cross-team ownership & governance, capacity planning
- Dual-track planning, go/no-go gates, risk mitigation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example idempotent handler
function handleEvent(event) {
  const id = event.id;
  if (hasProcessed(id)) return;
  markProcessed(id);
  process(event);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success and adapt if MTTR targets aren’t met?
- What would trigger a rollback vs. feature flag deprecation in production?

